    <edge source="G01" target="D01"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Cited prior work states that loss of function of the three named genes leads to CCM formation; &#x27;these genes&#x27; refers to the three genes named in the preceding sentence. Stated for CCM directly, not propagated from FCCM.</data><data key="evidence">These prior studies demonstrated that the products of the three genes (KRIT1, CCM2, and PDCD10) are associated with CCM form complexes. Loss of function (LOF) of these genes leads to the formation of CCM. || In the 2-hit model, biallelic LOF in CCM genes, achieved by one somatic mutation in heterozygous germline mutation or two somatic mutations in wild-type gene, is required to initiate CCM formation.</data><data key="location">b0011 (Body / Introduction; chars 640-844) || b0098 (Body / Discussion; chars 1199-1396)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G01" target="D02"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">both</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">The causal claim is the authors&#x27; background statement (b0003/b0011), not tested by this study. This study&#x27;s own results are consistent with it: 9 of 21 FCCM patients carried heterozygous KRIT1 variants classified LP or previously reported (Table 2), the K7 variant co-segregated with MRI-confirmed CCM in the mother, and the KRIT1 group showed a steeper age-lesion correlation (r = 0.75). A retrospective case series cannot by itself establish causality.</data><data key="evidence">Familial CCM (FCCM) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in three genes, KRIT1, CCM2, and PDCD10. || Cohort 1 consisted of nine patients with KRIT1 variants || The family members of one patient with a KRIT1 variant (K7), and two patients with PDCD10 variants (P1 and P2), received genetic testing, and four family members with the same variant as the proband were detected. Furthermore, these family members had CCM lesions confirmed by brain MRI. || the burden of the KRIT1 group was aggravated faster than that of the Mutation not detected group</data><data key="location">b0011 (Body / Introduction; chars 201-315) || b0028 (Body / Result / Study population; chars 0-55) || b0070 (Body / Result / Detailed clinical, genetic data, and age-related disease burden of gene-confirmed FCCM patients; chars 154-441) || b0104 (Body / Conclusion; chars 372-468)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G02" target="D01"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Cited prior work states that loss of function of the three named genes leads to CCM formation; &#x27;these genes&#x27; refers to the three genes named in the preceding sentence. Stated for CCM directly, not propagated from FCCM.</data><data key="evidence">These prior studies demonstrated that the products of the three genes (KRIT1, CCM2, and PDCD10) are associated with CCM form complexes. Loss of function (LOF) of these genes leads to the formation of CCM. || In the 2-hit model, biallelic LOF in CCM genes, achieved by one somatic mutation in heterozygous germline mutation or two somatic mutations in wild-type gene, is required to initiate CCM formation.</data><data key="location">b0011 (Body / Introduction; chars 640-844) || b0098 (Body / Discussion; chars 1199-1396)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G02" target="D02"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">both</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Background causal statement plus this study&#x27;s detection of CCM2 variants (both previously reported) in 2 FCCM patients. No family testing for CCM2 carriers. The CCM2 group&#x27;s negative age-lesion correlation (b0094) is attributed by the authors to possible detection bias (b0101), not to absence of a relation.</data><data key="evidence">Familial CCM (FCCM) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in three genes, KRIT1, CCM2, and PDCD10. || two with CCM2 variants || The CCM2 group comprised one patient with a nonsense mutation and one with a missense mutation.</data><data key="location">b0011 (Body / Introduction; chars 201-315) || b0028 (Body / Result / Study population; chars 57-79) || b0070 (Body / Result / Detailed clinical, genetic data, and age-related disease burden of gene-confirmed FCCM patients; chars 838-933)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G03" target="D01"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Cited prior work states that loss of function of the three named genes leads to CCM formation; &#x27;these genes&#x27; refers to the three genes named in the preceding sentence. Stated for CCM directly, not propagated from FCCM.</data><data key="evidence">These prior studies demonstrated that the products of the three genes (KRIT1, CCM2, and PDCD10) are associated with CCM form complexes. Loss of function (LOF) of these genes leads to the formation of CCM. || In the 2-hit model, biallelic LOF in CCM genes, achieved by one somatic mutation in heterozygous germline mutation or two somatic mutations in wild-type gene, is required to initiate CCM formation.</data><data key="location">b0011 (Body / Introduction; chars 640-844) || b0098 (Body / Discussion; chars 1199-1396)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G03" target="D02"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">both</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Background causal statement; this study detected PDCD10 variants in 2 FCCM probands and the same variants in three MRI-positive relatives (P1 father; P2 two sons). The early-onset tendency is background (b0101) plus a non-significant trend here (b0094: correlation not significant in PDCD10 group).</data><data key="evidence">Familial CCM (FCCM) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in three genes, KRIT1, CCM2, and PDCD10. || two with PDCD10 variants || The family members of one patient with a KRIT1 variant (K7), and two patients with PDCD10 variants (P1 and P2), received genetic testing, and four family members with the same variant as the proband were detected. Furthermore, these family members had CCM lesions confirmed by brain MRI. || PDCD10 variant patients usually have early onset and severe manifestations.</data><data key="location">b0011 (Body / Introduction; chars 201-315) || b0028 (Body / Result / Study population; chars 81-105) || b0070 (Body / Result / Detailed clinical, genetic data, and age-related disease burden of gene-confirmed FCCM patients; chars 154-441) || b0101 (Body / Discussion; chars 871-946)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G04" target="D02"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">association</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Cited prior study (ref. 15) reporting an association; the wording is hedged (&#x27;proposed&#x27;), and it was not tested in this cohort (ZPLD1 was not on the gene panel).</data><data key="evidence">F. Gianfrancesco et al. proposed that ZPLD1 mutation is associated with FCCM. 15</data><data key="location">b0098 (Body / Discussion; chars 1032-1112)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G05" target="D03"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">association</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">human</data><data key="reason">Cited prior study (ref. 16) reports GOF MAP3K3 mutations in sporadic CCM patients. Recorded as association, because the text reports mutations found in patients, not a tested causal mechanism. Not propagated to CCM (D01) or FCCM (D02).</data><data key="evidence">Moreover, gain-of-function (GOF) mutations in MAP3K3, encoding MEKK3, have been confirmed in sporadic CCM patients. 16 || LOF in CCM genes and GOF in MAP3K3 are mutually exclusive, and the researchers suggested that CCM complexes suppress the activity of MEKK3.</data><data key="location">b0098 (Body / Discussion; chars 1397-1515) || b0098 (Body / Discussion; chars 1516-1655)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G06" target="D01"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">animal model</data><data key="reason">Cited preclinical work: heterozygous loss of the animal gene induces a CCM model. Scope is an animal model of unspecified species; this is not human genetic evidence and the edge is marked species_scope = animal model.</data><data key="evidence">Some preclinical studies have demonstrated that the CCM animal model induced by Krit1, Ccm2, or Pdcd10 heterozygous loss in the immediate postnatal period, tended to harbor lesions in the hindbrain. 17 , 18</data><data key="location">b0099 (Body / Discussion; chars 280-486)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G07" target="D01"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">animal model</data><data key="reason">Cited preclinical work: heterozygous loss of the animal gene induces a CCM model. Scope is an animal model of unspecified species; this is not human genetic evidence and the edge is marked species_scope = animal model.</data><data key="evidence">Some preclinical studies have demonstrated that the CCM animal model induced by Krit1, Ccm2, or Pdcd10 heterozygous loss in the immediate postnatal period, tended to harbor lesions in the hindbrain. 17 , 18</data><data key="location">b0099 (Body / Discussion; chars 280-486)</data></edge>
    <edge source="G08" target="D01"><data key="status">supported</data><data key="relation_type">causal</data><data key="source_scope">background</data><data key="species_scope">animal model</data><data key="reason">Cited preclinical work: heterozygous loss of the animal gene induces a CCM model. Scope is an animal model of unspecified species; this is not human genetic evidence and the edge is marked species_scope = animal model.</data><data key="evidence">Some preclinical studies have demonstrated that the CCM animal model induced by Krit1, Ccm2, or Pdcd10 heterozygous loss in the immediate postnatal period, tended to harbor lesions in the hindbrain. 17 , 18</data><data key="location">b0099 (Body / Discussion; chars 280-486)</data></edge>
  </graph>
